From f4bf50698087a96f9a06dbe84c7426a4e70f9e0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Liam Fucich <liam.fucich@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:04:38 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Xkntt: wire the extension into the build, the hart and
 the disassembler

The integration half of the previous commit.  Every hunk here touches a file
Spike already owned, so this is where a rebase onto new upstream will conflict.

riscv/encoding.h gains one #include.  That file is generated from riscv-opcodes
upstream, so keeping our DECLARE_INSNs in a separate header keeps the conflict
surface to a single line rather than a block inside a 6000-line generated file.
riscv.mk.in's get_opcode has to look in both headers as a result, hence the
encoding_hdrs variable and grep -h.

riscv/insn_template.h includes xkntt.h so the arithmetic helpers are visible in
every instruction body without each one repeating the include.

The hart owns the coprocessor (processor_t::kntt) because the kntt.* control
instructions reach it directly; sim_t additionally maps it onto the bus at
KNTT_BASE so the data plane is ordinary lw/sw.  attach_kntt() is called from
both constructor paths -- the no-dtb path returns early, so one call site would
have silently covered only the DTS path.  Only hart 0's coprocessor is mapped:
the SoC has one.

The disassembler entries are not cosmetic.  --log-commits is the artifact plan
A5's cosimulation differ consumes, and a differ that can only print a bare word
for the instruction that diverged is much harder to debug against.

Instructions are registered unconditionally, like every other non-overlapping
instruction in Spike; the require_extension(EXT_XKNTT) in each body does the
gating, so --isa=rv32i_zicsr_zicntr traps on all ten.

Verified: the Xkntt regression passes, and the extension was fault-injected
four ways -- kbfgs with the plan's wrong sign, a corrupted forward butterfly in
the coprocessor, require_extension removed from kmm, and MASK_KNTT_STAT widened
to ignore its reserved fields.  All four were caught.
